---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import { ocean } from '../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import Interlude from '../../components/Interlude.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Transits', href: '/explore/transits/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Transits, the astrology of timing"
  description="Transits describe relationships between the moving sky and a birth chart. Learn how astrologers use them for timing, why one transit can show up in many ways, and where interpretation begins."
  path="/explore/transits/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
      <h1>Transits: the astrology of timing</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A natal chart is calculated for one moment. The sky keeps moving. Transits describe the
        new relationships the moving planets form with the positions in a birth chart, adding a
        changing layer of timing to a chart that otherwise stays fixed.
      </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.currents.src}
        srcset={ocean.currents.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.currents.alt}
        width={ocean.currents.width}
        height={ocean.currents.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>A transit describes a condition, not a script</h2>
      <p>
        A transit can be very specific astrologically and still have more than one possible
        expression in a life. Saturn contacting the Midheaven, for example, may coincide with a
        period of added responsibility, restructuring, recognition, delay, commitment, or a change
        in how someone relates to vocation and public life. Which expression matters depends on the
        natal chart, the other timing factors, and what is actually happening for that person.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is one reason I am wary of transit interpretations that jump directly from a planetary
        contact to a promised event. Technique can narrow the field. It does not remove the need
        for context and synthesis.
      </p>
      <h2>Different cycles move at different speeds</h2>
      <p>
        The Moon moves quickly and makes repeated contacts throughout a month. Mercury, Venus, the
        Sun, and Mars work on shorter cycles than Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto. The
        slower planets can remain within range of an important natal point for much longer, often
        returning to it during retrograde motion. That difference in duration is one reason
        astrologers tend to give slower transits more weight when looking at longer chapters of life.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cycles themselves are part of what I find so compelling: the same planets keep moving,
        repeating rhythms we can calculate, while no two people meet those cycles through exactly
        the same chart or life.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Interlude
    image={ocean.dawnWave}
    eyebrow="Timing"
    heading="The sky repeats its cycles. Your life does not repeat in exactly the same way."
    body="A transit gives us a relationship to examine. The natal chart, the rest of the sky, and your lived circumstances help tell us which possibilities deserve attention."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>About retrogrades</h2>
      <p>
        Retrograde motion is an apparent reversal caused by the changing positions of Earth and the
        other planet; the planet does not literally reverse its orbit. Astrologers have long given
        retrograde periods interpretive significance, but that does not make every delay, typo, or
        bad day the fault of Mercury. <a href="/explore/retrogrades/">Retrogrades deserve more
        nuance than that.</a>
      </p>
      <h2>How transits become personal</h2>
      <p>
        A general sky event tells us what is happening overhead. A personal transit asks where that
        event is contacting your natal chart, what else is active at the same time, and whether the
        symbolism fits what is actually unfolding. That is the movement from general astrology to
        individualized interpretation.
      </p>
      <p>
        In a reading, I may see several plausible ways a transit could be expressing itself. I can
        explain what I am seeing and ask questions rather than pretending the chart has already told
        me which version of the story is yours. The conversation helps us get more specific.
      </p>
      <h2>Using timing without giving away your authorship</h2>
      <p>
        Timing can be useful when it helps you recognize what is developing, understand why a period
        feels different, or consider when a particular issue may be more active. It is less useful
        when it becomes a reason to stop making your own decisions. Astrology can add another layer
        of perspective to timing; it does not have to become a command.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want to see the collective sky rather than a personal chart, visit
        <a href="/current-sky/">Current Sky</a>. For individualized timing, established clients can
        use <a href="/readings/solar-return/">Solar Return</a>,
        <a href="/readings/life-changes/">Life Changes</a>, or
        <a href="/readings/monthly-transits/">Monthly Personal Transits</a> depending on the question.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="General sky. Particular chart. Particular life."
    body="If your timing question has become personal, the Reading Finder can account for whether you are new or returning and point you toward the right place to begin."
    buttonLabel="Use the Reading Finder"
    buttonHref="/reading-finder/"
    secondaryLabel="Keep following Current Sky"
    secondaryHref="/current-sky/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
